			"contents": "<div class=\"Story_container\"><div class=\"Story_data\"><div class=\"Story_title\"><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193416\"><span>Tell HN: I unknowingly broke the PHP RCE embargo. I am sorry</span></a></div><div class=\"Story_meta\"><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193416\">738 points</a></span><span class=\"Story_separator\">|</span><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=petecooper\"><span>petecooper</span></a></span><span class=\"Story_separator\">|</span><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193416\">3 days ago</a></span><span class=\"Story_separator\">|</span><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193416\">266 comments</a></span><div class=\"Story_comment\"><span>I posted here about the then-upcoming PHP RCEs related to CVE-2026-109618 &amp; CVE-2026-109619. I appreciate this has had considerable knock-on effects with the schedule for co-ordinated disclosure, and created additional workload.<p>I am truly sorry. I take full responsibility for my actions.</p><p>It was not remotely my intention to cause any of the subsequent side effects.</p><p>At the time of posting, I didn't know about any embargo. My contact had told me there was no embargo, and her contact had told her the same thing.</p><p>As someone who follows the 4-weekly PHP release cycle and compiles it from source, my intention was to alert others in a similar position to be aware of the importance/urgency of the updates.</p><p>That clearly backfired in a pretty wild fashion. I have learned my lesson.</p></span></div></div></div></div>",
			"retrieved": 1792763333233
		},
