		{
			"id": "link-d2d147873d73c857a64872018464431a",
			"title": "Ask HN: If the models are so good, why doesn't OpenAI just start 100 companies?",
			"link": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997",
			"contents": "<div class=\"Story_container\"><div class=\"Story_data\"><div class=\"Story_title\"><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997\"><span>Ask HN: If the models are so good, why doesn't OpenAI just start 100 companies?</span></a></div><div class=\"Story_meta\"><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997\">660 points</a></span><span class=\"Story_separator\">|</span><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ex-aws-dude\"><span>ex-aws-dude</span></a></span><span class=\"Story_separator\">|</span><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997\">3 days ago</a></span><span class=\"Story_separator\">|</span><span><a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997\">743 comments</a></span><div class=\"Story_comment\"><span>Title is a bit of an exaggeration but the question is more: if the frontier models are so good, why even sell them as your primary money maker?<p>Couldn't OpenAI or Anthropic make a lot more money by just starting companies themselves that leverage the models and building their own products?</p><p>Is selling tokens really the best strategy?</p><p>Especially with commodification and prices declining wouldn't that indicate there is more money to be made on the application-side of the models rather than the utility side</p></span></div></div></div></div>",
			"retrieved": 1792858451928
		},
